				<th>Level</th>
				<th>Source</th>
				<th>Message</th>
			</tr>
		</thead>
		<tbody>
			{#each logRows as row}
				<tr data-tone={row.tone}>
					<td>{row.time}</td>
					<td>{row.level}</td>
					<td>{row.source}</td>
					<td>{row.message}</td>
				</tr>
			{/each}
		</tbody>
	</table>
</ReferencePreview>

## Colors 400–600: borders

Borders define component hierarchy and interaction.

<DocsTable columns={stepColumns} rows={borderRows} />

<ReferencePreview>
	<div class="border-states">
		<div class="border-state" data-state="default">Default</div>
		<div class="border-state" data-state="hover">Hover</div>
		<div class="border-state" data-state="active">Active</div>
	</div>
</ReferencePreview>

## Color opacity

To render a border token at 60% strength, mix the color with transparency. This changes only the border color; using the `opacity` property would fade the entire element and its content.

```css
.subtle-border {
  --border-opacity: calc(var(--ds-opacity-60) * 100%);
  border-color: color-mix(
    in oklch,
    var(--ds-border) var(--border-opacity),
    transparent
  );
}
```

Keep the composed value local for a one-off treatment. If the same visual role repeats across the product, promote it to an application-level semantic variable:

```css
:root {
  --app-border-subtle: color-mix(
    in oklch,
    var(--ds-border) calc(var(--ds-opacity-60) * 100%),
    transparent
  );
}
```

<ReferencePreview>
	<div class="alpha-border-example">
		<div class="alpha-border-example__item" style="--example-border-opacity: calc(var(--ds-opacity-100) * 100%)">100%</div>
		<div class="alpha-border-example__item" style="--example-border-opacity: calc(var(--ds-opacity-60) * 100%)">60%</div>
		<div class="alpha-border-example__item" style="--example-border-opacity: calc(var(--ds-opacity-30) * 100%)">30%</div>
	</div>
</ReferencePreview>

## Color 700: emphasis

Step `7` is the action/accent color for fills and highlighted UI elements.

<DocsTable columns={stepColumns} rows={emphasisRows} />

<ReferencePreview>
	<div class="metric-chart">
		{#each chartRows as row}
			<div class="metric-chart__row" style={`--metric-color: var(--ds-emphasis-${row.tone})`}>
				<span>{row.label}</span>
				<div class="metric-chart__track">
					<div class="metric-chart__value" style={`width: ${row.value}%`}></div>
				</div>
				<span>{row.value}%</span>
			</div>
		{/each}
	</div>
</ReferencePreview>

## Colors 800–900: text and icons

Step `8` (`--ds-foreground-muted-*`) is the default for colored text and icons. Despite the token name, it is not limited to secondary or disabled content. Step `9` (`--ds-foreground-*`) is the stronger option for text that needs emphasis; use it sparingly.

<DocsTable columns={stepColumns} rows={textRows} />

<ReferencePreview>
	<div class="event-list">
		{#each eventRows as event}
			<div class="event-item" data-tone={event.tone} data-emphasized={event.emphasized}>
				<span class="event-item__dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>
				<div class="event-item__content">
					<p class="event-item__title">{event.title}</p>
					{#if event.description}<p class="event-item__description">{event.description}</p>{/if}
					{#if event.meta}<p class="event-item__meta">{event.meta}</p>{/if}
				</div>
			</div>
		{/each}
	</div>
</ReferencePreview>

## Rules of thumb

- Use semantic `--ds-*` variables in components instead of palette primitives.
- Use `--ds-background` (`gray-0`) as the primary canvas and `--ds-background-secondary` (`gray-50`) for layered sections.
- For colored text, default to step `8` (`--ds-foreground-muted-*`), then escalate to step `9` (`--ds-foreground-*`) only when needed.
- Use step `7` for actions, not paragraph text.

## When to depart from the scale

Depart from the scale when readability or hierarchy requires it.

- `-100` can be an active background on compact elements if it improves clarity.
- You can swap gray foreground/background tokens to create intentionally high-contrast controls.

<ReferencePreview>
	<div class="contrast-example">
		<div>Gray swap contrast</div>
	</div>
</ReferencePreview>

## Color-vision presets

Color-vision presets change status colors without changing the selected accent.

Switch the interface theme to compare the status mapping below:

<ReferencePreview>
	<div class="status-palette">
		<div class="status-sample" data-tone="green"><span aria-hidden="true"></span>Success</div>
		<div class="status-sample" data-tone="blue"><span aria-hidden="true"></span>Info</div>
		<div class="status-sample" data-tone="orange"><span aria-hidden="true"></span>Warning</div>
		<div class="status-sample" data-tone="red"><span aria-hidden="true"></span>Error</div>
	</div>
</ReferencePreview>

Color remains a supporting cue. Components must also communicate status through text, icons, or structure. See [Theming](/docs/theming/#theme-presets) for setup and persistence.

